\section*{Uniform variance-influence bound for hypergraph properties}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Uniform hypergraph influence}: For fixed uniformity $r\ge3$, the paper claims a variance-to-influence inequality with denominator $(\log n)^{r/(r-1)}$ for all biases and all relabeling-invariant Boolean properties. Increasing properties therefore have the corresponding sharp threshold width.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For each fixed $r\ge3$, every $n\ge r$, every $0<p<1$, and every vertex-permutation-invariant Boolean property $f$ of an $r$-uniform hypergraph on $[n]$, the manuscript claims \[\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\le\frac{C_r}{(\log n)^{r/(r-1)}}I_p(f),\qquad I_p(f)=\sum_{e\in\binom{[n]}r}\mathbb P_p\bigl(f(x_{e\leftarrow1})\ne f(x_{e\leftarrow0})\bigr).\] The product measure is Bernoulli$(p)$ on hyperedges. Influence here contains no additional factor $p(1-p)$, and monotonicity is not required.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Uniform hypergraph influence}: Restrictions leave every hyperedge touching a random vertex block free, so vertex permutations still create large free-edge orbits. The restricted Fourier estimate controls low degrees uniformly in the bias. An $r$-uniform degree argument lifts this to the original spectrum, while influence handles its high-degree remainder; monotone differentiation supplies the threshold consequence.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Uniform hypergraph influence}: The graph paper proves a uniform log-square sharp-threshold width; the separate hypergraph paper proves Var\_p(f)<=C\_r I\_p(f)/(log n)\textasciicircum{}(r/(r-1)) for every relabeling-invariant r-uniform hypergraph property, r>=3.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 186 describes Lean coverage for the graph-property threshold width; the hypergraph refinement is not part of this selection.

\textit{Uniform hypergraph influence}: No manuscript-specific Comparator is listed in the pinned inventory.

The documented selection does not cover the entire manuscript assertion relevant here.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

\subsection{Sources}
